    "reason": "English 'review' (noun) is highly polysemous, encompassing meanings such as 'critical appraisal' (e.g., movie review), 'formal assessment' (e.g., policy review), and 're-examination' (e.g., review of a decision). Japanese '確認' (kakunin) primarily means 'confirmation,' 'verification,' or 'checking for correctness.' While it can align with the 'checking' aspect of 'review' (e.g., reviewing a document for errors), it does not cover the other common and distinct senses of the English word. Therefore, '確認' is substantially narrower than the general English noun 'review' and would not be a natural or appropriate translation for a general learner across all contexts.",
